    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data regarding thermal management in high-performance braking systems. Specifically, I'm comparing the heat dissipation rates of carbon-ceramic discs versus semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles. Can you explain the primary mechanisms of heat soak in these materials and how they contribute to brake fade during heavy use?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Heat soak in braking systems primarily occurs through conduction, where intense heat from friction transfers into the surrounding components like calipers and air. In carbon-ceramic discs, high thermal conductivity allows for efficient heat rejection, whereas semi-metallic pads can retain heat more readily in their porous structure. This retained heat lowers the operating temperature of the friction material, reducing chemical reactivity and leading directly to brake fade.",
      "source": "target",
