    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also seeing some inconsistent data in our simulation regarding how the material behaves when it reaches extreme peak temperatures—it's almost as if the thermal stability is acting up or behaving unpredictably under certain stress loads. Does that instability usually stem from a failure in the material's structural integrity, or is it more about the interface between the pad and the disc?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unpredictable behavior at extreme temperatures often involves a complex interplay between material limitations and interface conditions. Structural failure in the pads or discs can manifest as degradation, but sometimes the issue lies in the variation of the tribological interface—the contact zone. Thermal expansion mismatches between the pad and disc, or changes in the chemical bond at the interface, can cause inconsistent friction coefficients under peak thermal load.",
      "source": "target",
