      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic friction coefficient behavior at high temperatures often stems from changes in the transfer film—that microscopic layer formed at the pad-disc interface. When thermal stress is excessive, this film can become unstable, vaporize, or chemically degrade, causing sudden jumps or drops in friction. If this instability crosses a critical threshold, the system can enter an unpredictable state where braking force cannot be reliably modulated, leading to severe operational failure.",
      "source": "target",
